**Xiaomi, Redmi and POCO phones can't show you who tried to unlock them.** HyperOS limits how many wrong PINs, patterns or passwords someone can enter, but it keeps no list of attempts you can check, and it never takes a photo. To find out who it was, install an app that's notified of failed unlocks, like [CrookCatcher](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.harteg.crookcatcher&referrer=utm_source%3Dwebsite%26utm_medium%3Dguide%26utm_campaign%3Dxiaomi-who-tried-to-unlock). It takes a photo with the front camera after a wrong PIN and emails it to you with the location. On a Xiaomi phone it needs two extra settings to keep working in the background, covered below.

## What your Xiaomi does after a wrong PIN

Xiaomi's security documentation says the system limits the number of times an incorrect password can be entered, and locks the phone after repeated wrong entries so nobody can guess their way in. In practice the lock screen shows a countdown, and the person has to wait until it ends before trying again.

Xiaomi doesn't publish how many guesses are allowed or how long each wait is, and it can differ between HyperOS versions. Here's [what Android itself does with failed unlock attempts](/blog/failed-unlock-attempts-android).

A countdown is the only sign you'll see that someone tried. It doesn't say who, when, or how many times.

## Xiaomi settings worth turning on

These don't show you who tried, but they make your phone harder to get into and easier to find.

### Find device (Xiaomi Cloud)

Xiaomi's own tracking service is called Find device.

**Go to:**
`Settings > Xiaomi Account > Xiaomi Cloud > Find device`

Turn it on, grant the permissions it asks for and confirm with your Xiaomi Account password. Location has to be on. If the phone goes missing, sign in at [i.mi.com](https://i.mi.com) with the same account and choose **Find device**. You can see the phone on a map, play a sound at full volume even in silent mode, lock it with **Lost mode** or erase it.

Xiaomi says none of this works if the phone isn't connected to the internet or the SIM card has been replaced.

### Google's Theft protection

Xiaomi phones with Google services also have Android's Theft protection.

**Go to:**
`Settings > Google > All services > Theft protection`

- **Theft Detection Lock:** locks the screen if the phone detects that someone snatched it and ran.
- **Offline Device Lock:** locks the screen soon after the phone goes offline.

You need a screen lock first. If a switch is grayed out, your model doesn't support that feature. Read more in [Android Theft Detection Lock: what it does and what it misses](/blog/theft-detection-lock).

Also make sure Google's **Find Hub** is on. It works alongside Xiaomi's Find device, and it's worth having both.

## What your Xiaomi can't tell you

- **Who tried.** No photo of the person.
- **When it happened**, or how many times.
- **Where the phone was** at the time.
- **An alert while it happens.**

Xiaomi's **App lock** (`Settings > Apps > App lock`) doesn't fill this gap either. It puts a password on individual apps, and Xiaomi's help page describes no option to photograph someone who gets it wrong.

## How to get a photo of whoever tries to unlock your Xiaomi

CrookCatcher fills exactly that gap. Android notifies apps with the **Device admin** permission each time an unlock fails. CrookCatcher uses that to take a photo with the front camera, record the location and an approximate address, and email both to you. Every attempt is also saved in the app with its time.

